> ```cs
> public interface IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther> : IEquatable<TOther> where TSelf : IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther>
> {
>     static abstract bool operator ==(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
>     static abstract bool operator !=(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
> }
> ```

Wouldn't this require `where TSelf : default where TOther : default` or something added to the constraints as well? Wouldn't that be a bit overkill on generic constraints? (Consider someone making a helper method that takes in a `T` constrained to this interface, they'd likely have to write out *all* those extra constraints to make the compiler happy. [In general, that's a good thing](https://ericlippert.com/2012/03/09/why-not-automatically-infer-constraints/), but it wouldn't feel great in this scenario.)

When in doubt, it could use the attribute like how it's used on `IEqualityComparer<T>`:
```cs
public interface IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther> : IEquatable<TOther> where TSelf : IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther>
{
    static abstract bool operator ==([AllowNull] TSelf left, [AllowNull] TOther right);
    static abstract bool operator !=([AllowNull] TSelf left, [AllowNull] TOther right);
}
```
(Well, I *thought* `IEqualityComparer<T>` used `[AllowNull]`, but now I'm in doubt.)

So I thought we already had this configured correctly, but generic NRT annotations are always wonky...

I think the definition needs to be either:
```csharp
public interface IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther>
    where TSelf : IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther>?
{
	static abstract bool operator ==(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
	static abstract bool operator !=(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
}
```
-or-
```csharp
public interface IEqualityOperators<TSelf, TOther>
    where TSelf : IEqualityOperators<TSelf?, TOther?>?
{
	static abstract bool operator ==(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
	static abstract bool operator !=(TSelf? left, TOther? right);
}
```

but I'm not positive which is correct and am waiting to hear back from some of the Roslyn team members. 

